  "text": "271: et al., 2015).\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\cdot) is abandoned.\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\cdot).\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\n279: But it shows no improvement.\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\n283: We leave it for future research.\n284: 5. Training Techniques\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\_goods\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\n292: Take ℓ2\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\n293: Only parameters of non-zero sparse features appearing in each mini-batch needs to be updated in the scenario of SGD based optimization methods without regularization.\n294: However, when adding ℓ2\\ell_{2} regularization it needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters for each mini-batch, which leads to extremely heavy computations and is unacceptable with parameters scaling up to hundreds of millions.\n295: Figure 3. Control function of PReLU and Dice.\n296: In this paper, we introduce an efficient mini-batch aware regularizer, which only calculates the L2-norm over the parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch and makes the computation possible.\n297: In fact, it is the embedding dictionary that contributes most of the parameters for CTR networks and arises the difficulty of heavy computation.\n298: Let 𝐖∈ℝD×K\\bm{\\mathrm{W}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{D\\times K} denote parameters of the whole embedding dictionary, with DD as the dimensionality of the embedding vector and KK as the dimensionality of feature space.\n299: Expand the ℓ2\\ell_{2} regularization on 𝐖\\bm{\\mathrm{W}} over samples\n300: (4)"
